A||||10||DJRice||pesky gas gauge||||5||
Z||000000||DJRice||12-23-1999||12:16 AM||||Ok so my brother has run his Fiero out of gas which apparently subsequently killed the fuel pump.  While the gas tank is out &lt;argh&gt; I want to go ahead and perform some type of adjustment to the gauge.  I looked up the gauge fix on the Fiero knowledge base, but are there any other ingenious fixes floating around out there....Other than filling up at just under 1/2 tank?<P>Thanks and Happy Holidays...||||reg||5
Z||000001||dogtired||12-23-1999||05:35 AM||||NOT Really & don't mean that to be a smart A$$ remark.<BR>It is generally the guage itself. Realizing these little cars aren't aircraft, therefore not needed to be that accurate. Actually in flight school they encourage you top off the tanks every time you land. Multi purpose. 1st was so you just wouldn't forget and run out of fuel, second was because the guages were so poor, you can't figure to the last gallon and third to minimize condensation in the tank.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Just some food for thought? Since we are traveling with less than 10 gal in the first place, filling up when it is 1/4 tank isn't such a bad idea? Hope this Helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||Tom88gt||12-23-1999||02:14 PM||||Just ran my Formula outa gas today.  It too had just under 1/4 left on the guage.  I'll take the advise and just keep it topped up.  By the way, I'm a pilot too.  But the Navy has this thing about only putting enough gas to do your mission.  I never liked it but a P-3 can almost double its weight with a full tank, and it does burn more per hour to take the extra fuel.  Still, I don't think the military should be this cheap.  When flying Cessnas I always top off.<P>Keep her topped off and flying down the road.  I will.<P>||||reg||11
Z||000003||Voyd||12-23-1999||04:05 PM||||do all Fieros have this problem? i have let mine go down to the last little bar before empty a few times and it was fine. maybe mine is accurate but will fail me at the worst possible moment...yep, better keep it topped off.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||12-23-1999||05:35 PM||||I think all the 4-bangers have this problem!  Both my 84 and 88 Coupes ran out of gas (one time each  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) with an indicated 1/8 tank left.  <P>But both my 86 SE V6 and my father-in-law's will run down to an indicated "E"...consequently, I never ran either of them dry.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||12-23-1999||05:46 PM||||Maybe its unique to the coupe body style.  Don't have a clue why it would be.  My Formula is a V-6 but in a coupe body.  Its probably just a hit or miss item.  More likely, typical GM quality control while installing the sensor.  Every car is an individual.||||reg||5
Z||000006||superXmotorsports||12-23-1999||08:29 PM||||I've had 2 this year that ran out at 1/4 tank indicated.  My friends 86 GT does also.  When I got into this gauge deal I learned that you can move the needle after changing the gauge to help compensate.  (eg run down to 1/4 tank remove clusters, remove needle and replace at "E") this seems to work, and is good timing if you are updating to the color gauges.  SXM.||||reg||3
Z||000007||theogre||12-23-1999||09:10 PM||||I'm one of the lucky ones I guess....... When mine says empty you bloody better buy fuel.<P>I ran mine out once. It was semi intentional. I knew if it ran out I was a couple blocks walking. It also let me test the volume of the tank. I have 10 usable gallons.||||reg||1
Z||000008||William Federle||12-24-1999||09:19 AM||||I usually check my mpg every time I fill up (it varies from 27 to 30 these days). I count<BR>on 10 gal. The most I've ever had on the trip odometer (which I zero when I fill up) is about 250 miles. I don't think I'd feel good about trying to go 300 miles unless my ileage really improves. I think that the gas gauge is not very accurate, but I've come to know about how much fuel I have left at 1/4 and 1/2 on the dial. I mainly go by the miles on the trip odometer though if I'm just putting highway miles on it. If the driving is city and highway, I go by the gauge or use the worst-case mpgs.<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-24-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula||12-24-1999||04:16 PM||||My Formula ran out of gas after 90 miles, It was COMPLETELY FILLED, $10 before the 90 mile mark. but, the chrysler LHS that parks beside me was also out of gas. My friends tell it had to have been cyphoned(?). but I find it hard to believe, because you couldn't run a tractor on the amount of gas a fiero holds. and I have the pop open gas lid.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DJRice||12-24-1999||09:06 PM||||Well, I performed the mod on the gas gauge and it works like a champ.  Its even at 1/4 tank with 2.5 gallons in it.  This particular car is a red 86 v6 Notchback.  My brother has ran it out of gas twice, and subsequently killed the Fuel Pump twice, but at least the replacement has a lifetime warranty. He had gone 215 miles on the tank and it registered 1/4 when it died. <P>As for taking the time to adjust the gauge, I figured that since I had to drop the tank anyway, might as well give the mod a try and post the results for those interested in it should anyone else have to drop their gas tank.  ||||reg||1
